Z||000000||MikeSchaefer||11-13-2000||07:08 PM||||Hi,  I know that Archie offers the 3 kit options when buying.  Which one would I need to install a simple 350?  I'm not doing A/C or anything, and it's a carburated engine.  The car it's going into will be an 84 automatic.  Will I need everything in the master build kit eventually or would the deluxe kit get me by?  Has anyone done this with good results (using the deluxe kit only that is).  I'm not a mechanic, but an older gentleman with experience installing engines (not in a fiero though) will be helping me.  I would ask Archie himself, but I already talked to him once today, he was very helpful.<BR>Thanks,<BR>Mike Schaefer <A HREF="http://surf.to/fiero" TARGET=_blank>http://surf.to/fiero</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Old Lar||11-13-2000||07:39 PM||||If you want more information about Archies kit, I'd suggest you keep talking to Archie.  Any other responses maybe hearsay or just misinformation about Archies kit.  If you already have a kit or are planning to buy one of his kits, Archie likes nothing better than to talk up his product.   One unhappy customer can really damage his business reputation. I expect that Archie would want all who use his kits to make the right choices up front and do the install properly.  From what I have heard, Archie bends over backwards helping owners of his kits assemble them correctly.||||reg||10
Z||000002||Zorba||11-14-2000||12:22 AM|||| Go with archie kit. You can't go wrong. It's well thought out and sturdy. He will help you out too. Putting it all together requires attention to detail and following instructions, precisely.||||reg||12
